# Every edge of every graph this model composes, one row per (graph, filing, from, to).
#
# One relation, not four spellings
#   `rank/cycle_space.sqlc`, `rank/decomposition.sqlc`, `algebra/decomposition.sqlc` and the
#   emitter all need the same union over `composition/parts.sqlc` and `units/conversions.sqlc`.
#   Written out by hand in each, four spellings of one relation are four chances to typo one, and
#   no law here could compare them: an expression written inline is not a left operand, so
#   nothing could count it.
#
# The node is an opaque id
#   That is the one compromise in this file. A layer graph's node is `(filing, layer)` and a unit
#   graph's node is `(unit)`, two different arities, and one relation carrying both must pick a
#   common type. Everything downstream of this relation counts (nodes, edges, components, two
#   dimensions), and a count needs only that distinct nodes have distinct ids. That is why the
#   flattening is legitimate here and was a defect in the emitter: drawing needs to join a node
#   back to the model, and a concatenation cannot be joined to anything.
#   `rank/graph_nodes.sqlc` attaches each graph's node facts at real grain and flattens
#   afterwards, which is the same idiom in the order that works.
#
# The separator is `/`, and nothing forbids one in a layer name
#   `pm:Layer/name` is an `xs:token`, so two distinct layers could flatten to one node, and both
#   the drawing and the measurement would collapse identically and agree. On this corpus the
#   flattening is injective by luck rather than by construction, and this is the one place that
#   has to be true rather than four.
#
# `filing` is the per-filing cut, not part of the edge's identity
#   `rank/decomposition.sqlc` asks whether cutting each graph by filing loses an edge, so it
#   needs the column; `cycle_space` takes the graph whole and ignores it. A caller that wants the
#   corpus-wide edge set takes `DISTINCT graph, from_node, to_node` and drops it.
#
# Probe
#   Count the distinct `(filing, layer)` pairs and the distinct flattened strings; they must be
#   equal, and a layer name containing `/` is what would make them differ.
-- composition/parts.sqlc and units/conversions.sqlc, each labelled with the graph it is an edge of.
SELECT 'layers' AS graph,
       p.composition                            AS filing,
       p.composition  || '/' || p.composed_layer AS from_node,
       p.part_filing  || '/' || p.part_layer     AS to_node
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
UNION ALL
SELECT 'units', c.filing, c.from_unit, c.to_unit
FROM (
    :compose(units/conversions.sqlc)
) c
